t"><article class=3D"style_learnContent__K5K7M"><header><h1>Chapter 9: Beyo=
nd the Resume</h1></header><p>Your resume is not the only thing that can ge=
t you to a recruiter call: cover letters, your LinkedIn profile and a few o=
thers are worth paying attention to.</p>
<h2 id=3D"linkedin-profile">LinkedIn Profile</h2>
<p>Resumes and LinkedIn profiles are like the yin and yang. You directly ap=
ply with a resume, and opportunities can come and find you with your Linked=
In profile. It=E2=80=99s smart to invest equally in both.</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a Yin and Yang symbol illustrating the inte=
rconnectedness of a resume and a LinkedIn profile in a job search.  The Yin=
 and Yang is composed of a black half labeled 'Resume' containing a small l=
ight blue circle, and a light blue half labeled 'LinkedIn profile' containi=
ng a small black circle.  Thick black curved arrows point from the Yin and =
Yang towards the left, labeled 'Applications to positions,' indicating that=
 both the resume and LinkedIn profile are used to apply for jobs.  Similarl=
y, light blue curved arrows point from the right towards the Yin and Yang, =
labeled 'Reachouts from recruiters,' showing that recruiters contact candid=
ates through both their resume and LinkedIn profile.  The overall design em=
phasizes the synergistic relationship between these two job-seeking tools, =
highlighting how they work together to generate applications and attract re=
cruiter attention." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"695" height=3D"259" decoding=
=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https://bytebytego.com/_next/image?url=3D=
%2Fimages%2Fcourses%2Ftech-resume%2Fp2-c7-beyond-the-resume%2F35-5E43GNUF.p=
ng&amp;w=3D3840&amp;q=3D75" style=3D"color: transparent;"></div></figure>
<p>The good news is, once you have spent time polishing one, you can reuse =
much of the content for the other. There are a few differences that are wor=
th keeping in mind with LinkedIn, though.</p>
<p>Most of these differences come from how recruiters use LinkedIn most of =
the time: by looking at the LinkedIn search view. They set out to find cand=
idates who match a specific skillset, in a certain location, and might narr=
ow down to other filters: companies, schools, and others. Here how recruite=
rs will see your profile in the LinkedIn recruiter search view:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a screenshot of a tech-resume search engine=
 results page displaying 152,384 results.  The left panel shows filter opti=
ons for refining the search:  'Job titles' (with options like 'Sales,' 'Acc=
ount Manager,' and 'Sales Director'), 'Locations' (e.g., 'Greater Chicago A=
rea,' 'Indianapolis, Indiana Area'), 'Skills' (including 'Sales,' 'Sales Ma=
nagement,' 'Account Management,' 'Enterprise Software,' and others), 'Compa=
nies' (allowing for adding company names or Boolean searches), and 'Schools=
.'  These filters are visually connected via implied arrows to the right pa=
nel, suggesting their influence on the displayed results. The right panel s=
hows search results, structured as follows:  (1) a headline section display=
ing the candidate's name (e.g., 'Finley Watts'), current title, location, a=
nd industry; (2) the current title and date; (3) past position titles and d=
ates; (4) education details, including school names (e.g., 'Lake Forest'); =
(5) a section implicitly showing the results filtered by a Boolean search f=
or specific skills and technologies; and (6) other filters (titles, locatio=
ns, companies) are implied to be used to further refine the search.  The Li=
nkedIn logo is visible next to one candidate's name, indicating a potential=
 integration with LinkedIn data.  The bottom of the page shows indicators l=
ike 'Company follower' and 'Being considered by 1 other team,' providing ad=
ditional context about the candidates." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"722" heig=
ht=3D"365" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https://bytebytego.com=
/_next/image?url=3D%2Fimages%2Fcourses%2Ftech-resume%2Fp2-c7-beyond-the-res=
ume%2F36-PDIBN6CD.png&amp;w=3D3840&amp;q=3D75" style=3D"color: transparent;=
"></div></figure>
<p><em>The LinkedIn recruiter search view, and the key details recruiters i=
mmediately glance at.</em></p>
<p>To optimize your =E2=80=9Csearch card", consider following these steps:<=
/p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Set a headline to represent what you <em>want</em> to be found =
for</strong>. This line is the first thing a recruiter will see, and it sho=
uld summarize the message you=E2=80=99d like to convey. Why should someone =
click on your profile? If you do not set anything to your headline, your cu=
rrent position will appear here.</li>
<li><strong>Tweak your current position</strong> to describe your role bett=
er. LinkedIn is not your resume, and your current position does not have to=
 be your official title, as per your work contract. For example, if you are=
 leading a team as a frontend developer, you could have your current title =
on LinkedIn say =E2=80=9CFrontend lead=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9CFrontend team l=
ead=E2=80=9D, even if your official title at work is "Software Developer". =
Similarly, you can change your current title to say =E2=80=9CBackend engine=
er specializing in Go=E2=80=9D if this is both what you do and how you'd li=
ke recruiters searching on LinkedIn to find you.</li>
<li><strong>Mention keywords in your summary and job descriptions</strong> =
that recruiters could search for. These include technologies, frameworks, a=
nd engineering practices.</li>
<li><strong>Use a professional-looking photo</strong> that makes you look g=
ood. A professional photo is one where your face can be seen clearly, with =
no one else in the picture. Note that you don=E2=80=99t <em>need</em> to ha=
ve any photo on your profile, especially as photos can encourage biases. Al=
so, no photo is better than a poor photo, where you look grumpy or unfriend=
ly.</li>
<li><strong>Have a summary that sells you.</strong> Unlike with your resume=
, where recruiters and hiring managers barely look at your summary, on Link=
edIn they are more likely to do so. As a hiring manager, I read the summary=
 section and the last one or two job descriptions. So go for a longer summa=
ry, up to two paragraphs, where you describe the value you can bring and yo=
ur motivation.</li>
<li><strong>It=E2=80=99s fine to be more verbose on your LinkedIn than you =
would on your resume</strong>. The nice thing about LinkedIn is you don=E2=
=80=99t have to fit everything on one page. In fact, with more content, you=
 could rank higher in search results than if you kept things concise.</li>
<li><strong>Omit work experience that doesn't support your professional car=
eer story.</strong> Similar to your resume, your LinkedIn profile should te=
ll a good story of your professional side. Remove positions that don=E2=80=
=99t strengthen this image. For example, if you did a part-time job years b=
ack that has nothing to do with your current profession, it probably will n=
ot add anything to your career. Don=E2=80=99t have this kind of noise.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>How recruiters do Boolean searches</strong></p>
<p>To optimize your LinkedIn profile for recruiter searches, let=E2=80=99s =
take a look behind the scenes on how recruiters search for candidates. They=
 use the technique called Boolean search, something that should ring famili=
ar to most developers. Using a series of AND, OR, and NOT operators, they f=
ilter details. Here are a couple of possible searches recruiters might use:=
</p>
<ul>
<li>Software developer: <strong>=E2=80=9Csoftware developer=E2=80=9D OR =E2=
=80=9Csoftware engineer=E2=80=9D</strong></li>
<li>Fullstack developer with React.js and Python experience:
<ul>
<li><strong>=E2=80=9DReact.js=E2=80=9D AND =E2=80=9CPython=E2=80=9D</strong=
> for a simple search</li>
<li><strong>(=E2=80=9Cdeveloper=E2=80=9D OR =E2=80=9Cengineer=E2=80=9D) NOT=
 =E2=80=9Cmanager=E2=80=9D AND React.js=E2=80=9D AND =E2=80=9CPython=E2=80=
=9D</strong> for a more sophisticated search</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Frontend developer with React or similar experience: <strong>(=E2=80=9C=
frontend developer=E2=80=9D OR =E2=80=9Cfront-end developer=E2=80=9D) AND (=
=E2=80=9Cjavascript=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Ctypescript=E2=80=9D) AND (=E2=80=
=9Creact=E2=80=9D OR =E2=80=9Creactjs=E2=80=9D OR =E2=80=9Cvue=E2=80=9D OR =
=E2=80=9Cvuejs=E2=80=9D OR =E2=80=9Cangular=E2=80=9D OR =E2=80=9Cangularjs=
=E2=80=9D)</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>Recruiters will often Boolean search for keywords that are in the job de=
scription that the hiring manager gives them. So taking this example from a=
 job description, highlighting possible keywords:</p>
<ul>
<li>Computer Science fundamentals in data structures, algorithm design, pro=
blem solving, and complexity analysis</li>
<li>Proficiency in at least one modern programming language such as C, C++,=
 Java, or Perl</li>
</ul>
<p>A recruiter could create these Boolean searches:</p>
<ul>
<li><em>Focus on languages:</em> <strong>=E2=80=9CC=E2=80=9D OR =E2=80=9CC+=
+=E2=80=9D OR =E2=80=9CJava=E2=80=9D OR =E2=80=9CPerl=E2=80=9D</strong></li=
>
<li><em>CS fundamentals and languages:</em> <strong>(=E2=80=9Cdata structur=
es=E2=80=9D OR =E2=80=9Calgorithm=E2=80=9D OR =E2=80=9Calgorithms=E2=80=9D =
OR =E2=80=9Cdesign=E2=80=9D) AND (=E2=80=9CC=E2=80=9D OR =E2=80=9CC++=E2=80=
=9D OR =E2=80=9CJava=E2=80=9D OR =E2=80=9CPerl=E2=80=9D OR =E2=80=9Cobject =
oriented=E2=80=9D)</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>If your profile contains these phrases, you=E2=80=99ll appear in the res=
ults. The closer the match=E2=80=94for example, when matching in the headli=
ne=E2=80=94and the closer you are in your network with the recruiter, the h=
igher you will rank on the search results.</p>
<p><strong>Optimizing LinkedIn when job searching</strong></p>
<p>When you are either actively job searching or are getting ready to start=
, consider the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Signaling you=E2=80=99re open for opportunities: don=E2=80=99t d=
o this in your headline.</strong> Do not add =E2=80=9CLooking for opportuni=
ties=E2=80=9D, =E2=80=9COpen for a new role=E2=80=9D or something similar i=
n your Headline. This won=E2=80=99t add much value, and it will take up val=
uable space that you could use to sell yourself and highlight your skills. =
Instead, activate the Open to Job Opportunities feature in your LinkedIn pr=
ofile:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a section of a webpage, likely within a res=
ume or profile settings area of a job search platform.  The top displays th=
e bold heading 'Let recruiters know you're open to opportunities,' followed=
 by explanatory text: 'Share that you're open and appear in recruiter searc=
hes matching your career interests.' Below this, a secondary explanatory te=
xt reads: 'We take steps not to show your current company that you're open,=
 but can't guarantee complete privacy.'  A hyperlink, 'Learn more,' is embe=
dded within this second text block. Finally, at the bottom, a toggle switch=
 labeled 'Yes' is presented, with a checkmark indicating that the option is=
 currently selected and a green 'Saved' label confirming the setting's pers=
istence. The overall arrangement is top-to-bottom, with the heading and exp=
lanatory text above the toggle switch and status indicator.  The informatio=
n flow is unidirectional; the user interacts with the toggle switch to cont=
rol whether their profile is visible to recruiters, and the platform provid=
es information about the implications of this choice." loading=3D"lazy" wid=
th=3D"516" height=3D"167" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https:/=
/bytebytego.com/_next/image?url=3D%2Fimages%2Fcourses%2Ftech-resume%2Fp2-c7=
-beyond-the-resume%2F37-OZMSY645.png&amp;w=3D3840&amp;q=3D75" style=3D"colo=
r: transparent;"></div></figure>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Always have a current position listed</strong>, even if you don=
=E2=80=99t currently have a job. LinkedIn ranks your profile lower when you=
 do not have a current position. <a href=3D"https://www.linkedin.com/in/kat=
hybernard/" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">Kathy Bernard</a>=
, who specializes in optimizing LinkedIn profiles, advises to always fill o=
ut something as content here=E2=80=94even if you don't have a job right now=
. As a developer, you can tailor this current position to showcase the tech=
nologies and languages you are an expert in. For a few more pointers, see h=
er article: <a href=3D"https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/unemployed-create-cur=
rent-role-linkedin-kathy-bernard/" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener norefe=
rrer">Unemployed? Create a =E2=80=9Ccurrent=E2=80=9D role on LinkedIn</a>.<=
/p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Add more headline details.</strong> Consider using the =E2=80=9C=
|=E2=80=9D separator character to add more information to your headline, su=
ch as key skills or technologies you=E2=80=99d like to be found for. For ex=
ample, a headline could be:<br>
<em>Full-stack dev with 5 years experience | Go, Java, JavaScript, React, S=
wing | Distributed systems, developer tooling &amp; fluid user interfaces.<=
/em><br>
A tailored headline will rank your profile higher with keyword searches and=
 grab the recruiter's attention better.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Connect with more people on LinkedIn, including recruiters</stro=
ng>. The closer you are connections-wise to the recruiter doing the search,=
 the higher your profile will rank. As you start your job search, do consid=
er adding current and former colleagues and recruiters who have previously =
reached out to you.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Consider updating your location when you have a definite destina=
tion in mind to move to</strong>. Location is often a filter that recruiter=
s use. If you are ready to move to a city or a country, you can update your=
 location, so you show up as a local candidate. Use good judgment, especial=
ly for cases when you=E2=80=99d need a visa to move. Updating a location wi=
ll most likely result in more inbound messages from local companies. Once y=
ou have a reachout, you can confirm if the company sponsors visas before in=
vesting more time in the process.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Use Boolean search when searching for jobs</strong>. It=E2=80=99=
s not just recruiters who can do Boolean searches: on job sites like Linked=
In, you can do so as well. You can use AND, OR and NOT for phrases, and par=
enthetical search. For example, you could use the following search string t=
o look for non-senior frontend jobs, for companies not building on top of t=
he Laravel framework:<br>
<strong>(frontend OR javascript OR react) AND NOT (senior or laravel)</stro=
ng><br>
Narrow your search to fewer opportunities that are a better match for what =
you are looking for, or your skillset. This will help you apply in a more f=
ocused way.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"github">GitHub</h2>
<p>There is no expectation of having GitHub on your resume, regardless of h=
ow many resumes and resume templates you might see these included in. As a =
hiring manager, I never explicitly look for either. It is a nice bonus to s=
ee it, though, and as a hiring manager, I frequently click through to check=
 it out.</p>
<p>Other hiring managers confirmed the same: they are more likely to click =
through and check out these profiles after the first scan. Recruiters are s=
omewhat less likely to do so: though the more technical the recruiter, the =
more inclined they are to take a look. Should you make it to the onsite, de=
velopers who would be interviewing you often click through and bring up the=
 projects as talking points on the interview. Which brings us to the most i=
mportant rule of linking to GitHub.</p>
<p><strong>Only include a GitHub link if there is something you are proud t=
o show off</strong> on these sites. Do not link to an empty Github profile =
or one that has poor code visible.</p>
<p>Clean up your GitHub profile to describe the work that people will find =
there if you do link it. Add proper READMEs to your main projects that give=
 context on what the project does, highlighting relevant code, setup, or co=
nfiguration.</p>
<p>As a hiring manager, here is what I look for when browsing through a Git=
Hub profile:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Context</strong>: what side projects has this person published?=
 What do they do? Do the pinned projects have a clear description on the pr=
ofile page? When clicking into the project, is there a clear README that de=
scribes the project: how to run it and how to test it?</li>
<li><strong>Code quality</strong>: how is this? Does it follow best practic=
es? Is naming decent, the code easy to read and clean?</li>
<li><strong>Testing</strong>: does the project have unit or integration tes=
ts? If they do, I know this person is aware of testing and has at least pla=
yed around with it.</li>
<li><strong>Contributing to larger projects</strong>: what larger open sour=
ce projects have they contributed to? I sometimes look at pull requests, an=
d the comments on the pull request, if it=E2=80=99s easy to find.</li>
<li><strong>Standout repositories</strong>: if there is a repository that h=
as a large number of stars, and lots of activity, how does this developer h=
andle pull requests, and how do they respond to issues? This can give some =
good, positive indication on their collaboration skills, similar to contrib=
utions to larger projects.</li>
<li>Things I don=E2=80=99t pay attention to are contributions in the last y=
ear, followers, numbers of repositories, and other statistics that have lit=
tle to do with code quality.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>To optimize your GitHub profile</strong> that you are linking on=
 your resume, follow these few steps:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Create a README for your profile</strong> and summarize things t=
hat a hiring manager should know about you, pointing to standout projects. =
To create a personal README, create a repo with the same name as your usern=
ame, and initialize it with a README:</p>
